   "source": [
    "### 5.2 Conv2D layer: forward and backward\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the forward pass is a matrix product $Y=AB$, the gradients follow from $\\delta A=\\delta Y B^{\\top}$ and $\\delta B=A^{\\top}\\delta Y$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{\\text{mat}}}=\\delta Y_{\\text{mat}}^{\\top}X_{\\text{col}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial b_f}=\\sum_{n,i,j}\\delta Y_{n,f,i,j},\\qquad \\delta X_{\\text{col}}=\\delta Y_{\\text{mat}}W_{\\text{mat}},\\qquad \\delta X=\\text{col2im}(\\delta X_{\\text{col}}).$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Written out, $\\delta X$ is a **full convolution of $\\delta Y$ with the spatially flipped kernel**, so the backward pass of a convolution is itself a convolution.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Weight sharing** (one filter reused at every position) is what makes a CNN parameter-efficient and translation-equivariant: a lesion detector fires wherever the lesion is. A layer has $F\\cdot C\\cdot k^2$ weights; the first layer here has $16\\cdot3\\cdot9=432$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**He initialisation**, $W\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\,2/\\text{fan}_{\\text{in}})$ with $\\text{fan}_{\\text{in}}=Ck^2$. For $y=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_ix_i$, $\\operatorname{Var}(y)=n\\operatorname{Var}(w)\\,\\mathbb E[x^2]$, and a ReLU zeroes half its inputs, so $\\mathbb E[x^2]=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Var}(\\text{pre-activation})$. Setting $\\operatorname{Var}(w)=2/n$ therefore keeps the signal variance constant from layer to layer."
   ]
  },
