    "### 5.4 ReLU, max pooling, global average pooling, dropout, dense\n",
    "\n",
    "| Layer | Forward | Backward |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **ReLU** | $y=\\max(0,x)$ | $\\delta x=\\delta y\\cdot\\mathbb 1[x>0]$ |\n",
    "| **MaxPool 2×2** | $y_{i,j}=\\max_{u,v\\in\\{0,1\\}}x_{2i+u,\\,2j+v}$ | gradient routed only to the arg-max of each window |\n",
    "| **Global avg pool** | $y_{n,c}=\\frac1{HW}\\sum_{i,j}x_{n,c,i,j}$ | $\\delta x_{n,c,i,j}=\\delta y_{n,c}/(HW)$ |\n",
    "| **Dropout** (inverted) | train: $y=\\mathbf m\\odot x/(1-p)$, $m_i\\sim\\text{Bernoulli}(1-p)$; test: $y=x$ | $\\delta x=\\mathbf m\\odot\\delta y/(1-p)$ |\n",
    "| **Dense** | $\\mathbf z=\\mathbf xW+\\mathbf b$ | $\\delta W=\\mathbf x^{\\top}\\delta\\mathbf z,\\ \\delta\\mathbf b=\\sum_n\\delta\\mathbf z_n,\\ \\delta\\mathbf x=\\delta\\mathbf z\\,W^{\\top}$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "* **ReLU** makes the network non-linear; without it, stacked convolutions collapse into a single linear filter. Its derivative is exactly 1 for active units, so gradients do not shrink as they pass back through many layers.\n",
    "* **Max pooling** halves the resolution, adds tolerance to small shifts, and makes later receptive fields grow faster.\n",
    "* **Global average pooling** replaces v1's `Flatten → Dense(100)`, which is the key change for a 120-image dataset:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\underbrace{8192\\times100+100}_{\\text{v1 head}}=819{,}300\\ \\text{parameters}\\qquad\\text{vs.}\\qquad\\underbrace{128\\times3+3}_{\\text{v2 head}}=387.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "  v1 put 92 % of its parameters into one layer that could memorise the training set. With GAP, each channel answers \"how much of pattern *c* is anywhere on this leaf?\", which suits lesions that can appear anywhere.\n",
    "* **Dropout** with $p=0.3$ stops the classifier relying on any single feature. Dividing by $1-p$ keeps $\\mathbb E[y]=x$, so inference needs no rescaling."
   ]
  },
